<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Utu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shamash</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Utu, called Shamash in Akkadian, was the Mesopotamian god of the sun, justice and truth. Each day he crossed the sky and saw all deeds, so he was invoked as judge and as protector of travellers; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>'s law code shows him handing the king authority. His chief temples, both named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/e-babbar">E-babbar</a>, were at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a>, and he was also worshipped in later Hatra and elsewhere in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/utu.jpg" alt="Utu" width="320" height="484" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Katolophyromai · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tablet_of_Shamash_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Utu, called Shamash in Akkadian, was the Mesopotamian sun god; the Sumerian name means &quot;Sun&quot;. He was believed to see everything that happened in the world each day, and was therefore responsible for justice and the protection of travellers. As a divine judge he could be associated with the underworld, though this is not attested before the Old Babylonian period. He and the weather god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a> were jointly regarded as gods of divination, especially extispicy. The moon god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanna">Nanna</a> (Sin) and Ningal were his parents, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inanna">Inanna</a> (Ishtar) his twin sister, and the dawn goddess Aya his wife.</p><p>He travelled each day through the sky from east to west in a solar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>, and later belief had him pass at night through a &quot;nether sky&quot; above the underworld. In art he is one of the few Mesopotamian deities identified with certainty, by a large saw and rays of light from his shoulders; he could also be shown handing the rod-and-ring symbol to human rulers. No myths centre on him, but he often appears as an ally. In versions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a> and in earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a> myths he helps the hero against Humbaba, and in accounts of Dumuzi's death he is asked for protection, though he can only delay it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The main cult centres were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a>, specifically Sippar-Ahrurum (Abu Habbah). In both the main temple of Utu and his spouse Aya was called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/e-babbar">Ebabbar</a>, &quot;shining white house&quot; in Sumerian; lesser temples of the same name stood at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a>. The Ebabbar at Sippar was renovated by rulers including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsu-iluna">Samsu-iluna</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a>, and that at Larsa by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> and Nebuchadnezzar II.</p><p>Evidence for worship in the third millennium BCE comes from the whole Akkadian-speaking area, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a> to Agade and the Diyala. Celebrations related to the sun god took place on the eighth, fifteenth, twentieth and possibly first day of each month. Prayers and literary formulas suggest that he was often invoked outside temples, presumably as an astral body. His name could also be used to write the names of many foreign solar deities, among them the Hurrian god Shimige.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shamash” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After this first city declined, a second city flourished from about 2550 to 2300 BCE, in the Early Dynastic period, with a royal palace and temples to Ishtar, <b>Shamash</b> and other gods.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mari">Mari · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Among the greatest were Enlil, king of the gods, An (Akkadian Anu), Enki (Ea), the moon god Nanna (Sîn), the sun god Utu (<b>Shamash</b>) and Inanna, goddess of sex and war.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesopotamian-religion">Mesopotamian religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>At its top a relief appears to show Hammurabi standing before the seated <b>Shamash</b>, the Babylonian sun god and god of justice, who wears the horned crown of divinity with flames spouting from his shoulders.</blockquote><a href="/work/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>At Sippar about 200 nadītu of the sun god <b>Shamash</b> lived in a walled cloister (gagûm).</blockquote><a href="/societies/old-babylonia">Social Hierarchy of Old Babylonia</a></li><li><blockquote>Nabu-apla-iddina restored the cult of <b>Shamash</b> at Sippar, commemorated on the Sun-god Tablet.</blockquote><a href="/state/dynasty-of-e">Dynasty of E</a></li><li><blockquote>At its centre stood a vast temple precinct to the sun god <b>Shamash</b>, called Maran ('our lord'), with shrines to Allat, Baalshamin and other deities.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hatra">Kingdom of Hatra</a></li></ul></section>
